Only problem with this argument is that afaict it winds up requiring a lot
of {bu} when it requires any (Pierre's example winds up being {re bu bi bu
.abu}), whereas {panzi be ny ci mei} is fixed by just one terminator. But
then, the problem with {panzi be ny ci mei} is that you get in a habit of
thinking of BY strings as being like KOhA, and ending when the lerfu end or
when boi appears...and then you're wrong, because what you thought were BY
strings are actually BY-and/or-PA strings. Which seems rather opposite to
the whole "regular" thing. (Even though it actually is regular, it doesn't
*feel* regular at all; does that count for anything?)

